I joined DeepMind in 2017, and I remember my manager describing Demis's most brilliant move as not relocating to Silicon Valley. This decision meant that for a 5-7 year period, every senior AI researcher in Europe who wanted to join a new or big AGI lab but didn't want to be 5000 miles away from home joined DeepMind. There was very little competition for talent during this time, leading to high retention rates.

Demis's opening moves were strategic: around 2010, he used video games to generate infinite training data for specific tasks. By 2014, he leveraged this data advantage to create impressive demonstrations that led to Google's acquisition, giving him a sustainable compute advantage. He also kept the team in London, which allowed DeepMind to attract top talent at lower salaries compared to competitors. As other labs built offices nearby, the geographical talent advantage diminished, but DeepMind still employs effective talent strategies. Overall, Demis is a master strategist, and his unconventional decisions have proven to be brilliant.

@HarryStebbings: "DeepMind stayed in London because it is better for talent than Silicon Valley. I saw London and the UK as having incredible talent from top universities like Cambridge, Oxford, Imperial, and UCL. There is a deep heritage of scientific breakthroughs and world-class thinkers. There was less competition for that talent, which made it a huge structural advantage for building DeepMind."

In one of the first comprehensive analyses on saturation, 60 popular benchmarks were studied, revealing that private test sets and open-ended tasks do not prevent saturation. Benchmarks are evolving measurement tools with lifecycles, not static artifacts.

@evaluatingevals: "As AI models improve, many benchmarks are becoming saturated and losing their ability to distinguish between models."

Check out the new @icmlconf paper: “When AI Benchmarks Plateau: A Systematic Study of Benchmark Saturation”.
